In article <nate.1237@psygate.psych.indiana.edu> nate@psygate.psych.indiana.edu (Nathan Engle) writes:
>    Just in case you were wondering, YES I make save files, and I do it 
>for the exact same reason I back up my hard disk. I could theoretically 
>spend the time to start a new character and recreate everything the way 
>it was, but why bother? To satisfy the dubious moral code of a bunch of 
>misguided computer weenies?  Sorry.  If we have such high moral fiber 
>then why are we sitting there playing Nethack instead of curing cancer 
>or paying off the national debt?

I do NOT make save files, and I encourage others not to either.  I do
not do this out of some sense of morals.  I do it because I think it makes
the game more fun.

If I have a save file, I won't really be afraid of anything.  If I die, so
what?  I think the element of fear makes hack much more exciting.  And each
game is different enough to make it interesting. (unlike other CRPG, ex. Ultima)
Why don't you just play in explore mode, if you don't want to die?

Perhaps we play the game for different reasons.  I don't "spend the time
to start a new character", I enjoy trying to keep him/her alive.

The difference between a movie and a game is the possibility of defeat and
the corresponding possibility of achievement.


